1. Wayu-Paxa-TTS-Edge (TTH engine "wayu", optional, runs locally)
   Model weights: https://huggingface.co/wayu-ai/wayu-paxa-tts-edge
   By Paxa Labs / Wayu Research (Kunat Pipatanakul, Potsawee Manakul, Warit Sirichotedumrong,
   Sittipong Sripaisarnmongkol, Pakorn Nathong, Phatrasek Jirabovonvisut).
   License of the weights: CC-BY-NC-4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/).
   Attribution is required and commercial use is NOT permitted. Using or downloading the weights also means
   agreeing to the Wayu Research Acceptable Use terms (https://www.wayuresearch.org/terms#acceptable-use).
   The weights are fetched from Hugging Face by the wayu_tts package on first use and are never stored in or
   shipped with this repository. Audio made with the "wayu" engine is therefore for non-commercial use only.
   The voices are synthetic: do not present the audio as a recording of a real person, and say that it is
   machine-generated wherever a listener could be misled.
   Paper: "Building and Evaluating Fixed-Voice Thai TTS from Synthetic Speech", arXiv:2609.03502 (2026).

   Inference code: wayu-tts, https://github.com/wayu-research/wayu-tts-inference
   (commit 07ab898d516649313926f0b843b809a962f993ad), Apache-2.0. Installed by the "tth" extra.
   It builds on Kokoro (https://github.com/hexgrad/kokoro, Apache-2.0), tltk and PyThaiNLP.

2. edge-tts (TTH engine "edge", optional fallback, online)
   https://github.com/rany2/edge-tts, LGPL-3.0, installed by the "tth" or "tth-edge" extra.
   It sends the text to the Microsoft Edge read-aloud service over the internet to make the audio; the service
   and its voices (for example th-TH-PremwadeeNeural) belong to Microsoft and follow Microsoft's terms.

3. Web demo speech (docs/index.html, "Speak replies (TTH)")
   Uses the browser's Web Speech API with a Thai voice already installed on the device. TER-AI ships no voice
   for it and sends nothing; how the device or browser makes the speech is up to its maker.

TTH does not use the Paxa Labs cloud API (Paxa TTS Flash).

SANKPUP (pictures from code, v0.11.0)
SANKPUP uses no third-party model or data inside this repository. The optional parts below are installed or
downloaded separately and keep their own licenses.

4. Gemma 4 (SANKPUP engine "gemma", optional, runs locally)
   Model weights by Google DeepMind: official QAT Q4_0 GGUF builds, e.g.
   https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-E2B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf
   License of the weights: Apache License 2.0 (https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/gemma_4_license).
   Downloaded on first use into ~/.cache/terai/models and checked against the published SHA-256.

5. llama-cpp-python (https://github.com/abetlen/llama-cpp-python), MIT. Installed by the "sankpup-gemma" extra.

6. resvg-py (https://github.com/baseplate-admin/resvg-py), MIT, wrapping resvg
   (https://github.com/linebender/resvg), Apache-2.0; Pillow (https://python-pillow.org), MIT-CMU.
   Installed by the "sankpup" and "sankpup-gemma" extras.

SANKPUP does not call any cloud API.

MuML 1.0 WA (music in the browser with the Web Audio API, v0.12.0)
Unlike TTH and SANKPUP, MuML 1.0 WA includes third-party-derived data in this repository:

7. Nottingham Music Database, as cleaned by jukedeck/nottingham-dataset
   Repository: https://github.com/jukedeck/nottingham-dataset (commit 0992bb6cd864f6d4b90d6663e08c0ef53ffaa08f)
   Original ABC files: http://abc.sourceforge.net/NMD/
   License: GNU General Public License v3.0 (the repository's LICENSE.md).
   Used for: the note table src/terai_router/muml_wa/data/markov.json and its web copy docs/muml_wa_table.js
   (counts of pitch, chord and rhythm transitions; no tune is stored). These two files are GPL-3.0; the full
   license text is next to each of them (LICENSE-GPL-3.0.txt). The code that reads them stays MIT.
   Since v0.15.0 also used for: docs/muml-1.0-int8.bin, the weights of the MuML 1.0 network trained from
   scratch on this dataset (int8, 3,421,372 bytes). GPL-3.0 like the table; see DATA_LICENSE.

The note writer in v0.12.0 was a seeded Markov chain ("markov-v1"). Since v0.15.0 the trained MuML 1.0 network
writes the notes ("muml-1.0-net": docs/muml_wa_net.js and src/terai_router/muml_wa/net.py, MIT code, our own
forward pass, no onnxruntime and no third-party model); Markov v1 stays only as the fallback when the weights
(or numpy, in Python) are missing. All sound is synthesized by the Web Audio API in the browser: no soundfont, no samples, no server, no cloud API.

MUML 1.0 HIQ (text-to-music audio, released in v0.13.0)
MUML 1.0 HIQ ships only TER-AI code (MIT) in this repository: the Thai/English prompt adapter and the
wrapper. The model and runtime below are downloaded or built separately and keep their own licenses.

8. ACE-Step 1.5 (turbo DiT "acestep-v15-turbo", VAE) by ACE Studio and StepFun
   https://huggingface.co/ACE-Step/Ace-Step1.5 , https://github.com/ace-step/ACE-Step-1.5
   License: MIT (code and weights). Not gated on Hugging Face.
   GGUF conversions used at run time: https://huggingface.co/Serveurperso/ACE-Step-1.5-GGUF (MIT).
   Downloaded on first use into ~/.cache/terai/muml-hiq/models (or $TERAI_MUML_HIQ_MODELS).

9. Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (ACE-Step 1.5 text encoder) by the Qwen team, Apache License 2.0
   https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (GGUF Q8_0 from the repository in 8).

10. acestep.cpp by ServeurpersoCom, MIT, built on ggml (MIT)
   https://github.com/ServeurpersoCom/acestep.cpp , pinned commit de858d8e1a94f0e1939cf9bdd8ff7d9631953077.
   Cloned and built by `terai muml --hiq --setup`.

11. Thai vocal "split" mode (`terai muml --hiq --lyrics ... --vocal-engine split`, since v0.13.0)
   ACE-Step renders an instrumental and the Thai lyrics are voiced by the local TTH engine, Wayu-Paxa-TTS-Edge
   (item 1 above). The Wayu weights are CC-BY-NC-4.0: commercial use is NOT permitted and Paxa Labs / Wayu
   Research must be credited. Every song made with --thai-vocal split contains that voice, so the song is for
   non-commercial use only, even though the ACE-Step part is MIT. The voice is synthetic: say that it is
   machine-generated wherever a listener could be misled.
   Optional pitch retuning (--split-style snap or melody) uses pyworld (WORLD vocoder by Masanori Morise,
   modified BSD license; https://github.com/JeremyCCHsu/Python-Wrapper-for-World-Vocoder), installed separately.

12. YuE2-3B by m-a-p (Multimodal Art Projection), default engine for songs with Thai lyrics
   (`terai muml --hiq --lyrics <Thai> [--vocal-engine yue2]`, since v0.13.0)
   Model: https://huggingface.co/m-a-p/YuE2-3B, VAE: https://huggingface.co/m-a-p/YuE2-Vae
   Licence of both: CC-BY-NC-4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/). Commercial use is NOT
   permitted and m-a-p must be credited. Every song made with the yue2 engine is for non-commercial use only;
   the CLI prints this warning before each render.
   GGUF conversions (Q5_K_M used by default, Q6_K, Q8_0; VAE F32), CC-BY-NC-4.0:
   https://huggingface.co/Serveurperso/YuE2-GGUF
   Runtime: yue2.cpp by ServeurpersoCom, MIT, built on ggml (MIT): https://github.com/ServeurpersoCom/yue2.cpp
   pinned at commit 8af01740686824ffd4a1839a457954f42e2a8ce7 (2026-10-16). `--setup` downloads the GGUF files
   and builds that commit; nothing is redistributed in this repository.

13. PyThaiNLP (https://github.com/PyThaiNLP/pythainlp), Apache License 2.0. Optional: when installed, the ACE-Step
   engine romanises Thai lyrics with it (`royin`). Installed separately by the user; not bundled.

MUML 1.0 HIQ does not fine-tune or redistribute these weights and does not call any cloud API.

TERAI1BETA (chat model and image reading, `terai chat` / `terai vision`, since v0.16.0)
TERAI1BETA is TER-AI's rules used as tools by Google's Gemma 4. No weights are fine-tuned or redistributed here.

14. Gemma 4 E2B (default) or E4B, by Google DeepMind, official QAT Q4_0 GGUF builds plus the vision projector
   (gemma-4-E2B-it-mmproj.gguf / gemma-4-E4B-it-mmproj.gguf): https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-4-E2B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf
   License of the weights: Apache License 2.0 (https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/gemma_4_license), the same weights
   as item 4. `terai chat --setup` downloads them on first use into ~/.cache/terai/models and checks the published
   SHA-256; they are never committed to this repository. Runtime: llama-cpp-python (item 5), installed by the
   "llm" extra. Use of Gemma also follows Google's Gemma terms and prohibited use policy linked from that page.

TTH-MTL (TTH engine "mtl", "TTH หลายภาษา", prototype, not released, optional, runs locally)
15. OmniVoice by k2-fsa (Han Zhu, Lingxuan Ye, Wei Kang, Zengwei Yao, Liyong Guo, Fangjun Kuang, Zhifeng Han,
   Weiji Zhuang, Long Lin, Daniel Povey)
   Code: https://github.com/k2-fsa/OmniVoice, Apache-2.0. Installed from PyPI ("omnivoice") by the "tth-mtl" extra.
   Model weights: https://huggingface.co/k2-fsa/OmniVoice (about 3.3 GB). The model card states: "The pre-trained
   model is licensed under the CC-BY-NC due to constraints from its training data (e.g., Emilia)."
   Commercial use is NOT permitted and attribution is required. The weights are downloaded from Hugging Face by
   the omnivoice package on first use and are never stored in or shipped with this repository. Audio made with
   the "mtl" engine is therefore for non-commercial use only.
   OmniVoice's own disclaimer prohibits unauthorized voice cloning, voice impersonation, fraud and scams. By
   default TTH-MTL clones only a synthetic Thai clip read by the Wayu engine (item 1), so Wayu's CC-BY-NC-4.0
   terms apply to that audio too; clone your own voice only with --ref-audio, and never someone else's without
   their consent.
   Paper: "OmniVoice: Towards Omnilingual Zero-Shot Text-to-Speech with Diffusion Language Models",
   arXiv:2604.00688 (2026).
   No audio from ElevenLabs or Gemini TTS was used to build or tune TTH-MTL: their terms forbid using output to
   train or improve other models and require users to be 18 or older.
